# What is NOT uploaded to a Vercel build.
#
# The CLI does not read `.gitignore`, so without this file `vercel deploy` uploaded whatever the
# deploying machine happened to have on disk — including every `packages/*/dist` and its
# `*.tsbuildinfo`. That is how production came to depend on one laptop: the web build resolves
# `@compass/*` through each package's `exports` map, which points at `dist`, so the deployed app was
# compiled against locally emitted JavaScript and type declarations rather than against this
# repository. A `dist` emitted by a `tsc` run that had errored still emits, only with its types
# degraded to `any`, and the symptom was a remote `next build` failing type check inside
# `apps/web/lib/health.ts` — a file nobody had touched.
#
# So build outputs are excluded here and `apps/web/vercel.json` builds the packages remotely instead.
# The upload is now the source tree: what deploys is what is committed.

# Build outputs — rebuilt remotely by the build command.
node_modules/
dist/
build/
.next/
coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo
.pglite/

# Secrets. The CLI ignores `.gitignore`, so these have to be named again here or a stray local
# environment file would be uploaded into the build.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local state that is not an input to the build.
.git/
.nous/
.vercel/
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
